---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import PageShell from '../../components/nav/PageShell.astro';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import {
  CARGO_ADD,
  CARGO_DEP,
  CRATE_VERSION,
  CRATE_RELEASED,
  CRATESIO_URL,
  DOCSRS_URL,
  MSRV,
  CRATE_URL,
} from '../../consts';
import { RUN } from '../../data/crosstalk';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Get started | DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'Run the crosstalk example, add the crate from crates.io, and choose the feature flags and build configuration.';

const run = `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/deepcausality-rs/deep_causality.git
cd deep_causality
${RUN.command}`;
const dep = `[dependencies]
deep_causality_quantum = "${CRATE_VERSION}"`;
const gitdep = `[dependencies]
${CARGO_DEP}`;
const qpu = `[dependencies]
deep_causality_quantum = { version = "${CRATE_VERSION}", features = ["qpu"] }`;
const nostd = `[dependencies]
deep_causality_quantum = { version = "${CRATE_VERSION}", default-features = false, features = ["no-std"] }`;

const features = [
  { name: 'std', on: true, what: 'The standard-library build. It enables std on every non-optional workspace dependency.' },
  { name: 'qcm', on: true, what: 'The quantum causal model slice and the QCL builder: CausalStructure, the Markov and decomposability checks, and the pipeline. It uses the causal graph, which needs std, so qcm implies std.' },
  { name: 'dem', on: true, what: 'Builds a DemModel from Stim detector-error-model text. It implies qcm and adds no dependency.' },
  { name: 'alloc', on: false, what: 'Allocation without the standard library. Both std and no-std imply it.' },
  { name: 'no-std', on: false, what: 'The bare-metal build. It implies alloc and switches every non-optional workspace dependency to its own no-std feature.' },
  { name: 'qpu', on: false, what: 'The QPU seam, the interface to a device: QpuSampler, the bridges from shots to Uncertain values, qpu_effect, evidence and shot budgets, and the in-process SimQpu. It adds one dependency, deep_causality_uncertain, and nothing network-facing or async.' },
];

const configs = [
  { cfg: 'std, hosted', flags: 'default', ok: true, note: 'All layers, including the QCL builder.' },
  { cfg: 'no-std with a heap', flags: 'default-features = false, features = ["no-std"]', ok: true, note: 'The operator, gate and verdict layers. qcm and dem are host only. The program must define a global allocator.' },
  { cfg: 'no-std on core alone', flags: '', ok: false, note: 'Without std the crate needs a heap.' },
];
---
<PageShell title={title} description={description} path="/start/">
  <header class="doc-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Get started</p>
    <h1>Run the example, then add the crate.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Version {CRATE_VERSION} is MIT licensed and was released on crates.io on {CRATE_RELEASED}. It
      needs Rust {MSRV} or newer.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="run">
    <h2>How do you see it work first?</h2>
    <p>
      Clone the repository and run the crosstalk example. It refuses a cyclic candidate, screens
      three others, prints the plan (cost 2 of the 5 that all four experiments cost, in the
      example’s own unit), runs the plan’s first experiment and names the survivor, H1. The first run
      compiles the crates it depends on; the example itself finishes in milliseconds.
    </p>
    <Code code={run} lang="bash" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p>
      <a href="/examples/qcl-crosstalk/">The example page</a> explains the output line by line.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="add">
    <h2>How do you add it to a project?</h2>
    <p>Run <code>cargo add</code>, or write the dependency into <code>Cargo.toml</code> yourself.</p>
    <Code code={CARGO_ADD} lang="bash" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>The equivalent <code>Cargo.toml</code> entry:</p>
    <Code code={dep} lang="toml" themes={SHIKI} />
    <p>
      To follow unreleased work, depend on the <code>main</code> branch:
    </p>
    <Code code={gitdep} lang="toml" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p>
      The API reference is on <a href={DOCSRS_URL}>docs.rs</a>, built with all features; the
      release is on <a href={CRATESIO_URL}>crates.io</a>. Every dependency of the crate is a
      workspace crate, so the crate has no external runtime dependency. The crate forbids
      <code>unsafe</code> code through the workspace lints, without exemption.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="features">
    <h2>Which features does it have?</h2>
    <p>
      The crate has six features, and <code>std</code>, <code>qcm</code> and <code>dem</code> are
      on by default. The <code>Cargo.toml</code> entry below the table turns on <code>qpu</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Default</th><th scope="col">What it turns on</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {features.map((f) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Feature"><code>{f.name}</code></td>
              <td data-label="Default">
                {f.on ? <span class="status status-validated">on</span> : <span class="status status-roadmap">off</span>}
              </td>
              <td data-label="What it turns on">{f.what}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Code code={qpu} lang="toml" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="layers">
    <h2>What else is in the crate?</h2>
    <p>
      The QCL builder sits on four layers that a program can also call directly. The API reference
      on <a href={DOCSRS_URL}>docs.rs</a> documents each one.
    </p>
    <dl class="deflist">
      <div>
        <dt>Operator layer</dt>
        <dd>
          A validated <code>DensityMatrix</code>, the Choi–Jamiołkowski isomorphism
          (<code>choi_from_kraus</code>, <code>kraus_from_choi</code>), channel application
          (<code>apply_kraus</code>, <code>apply_choi</code>), and the dense kernels the checks are
          built from (<code>partial_trace</code>, <code>embed_on_legs</code>,
          <code>matrix_commutator</code>).
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Gate kernels</dt>
        <dd>
          Born probability, expectation value, gate application, commutator and fidelity, each
          with a wrapper that lifts it into the causal monad and sends a failure to the error
          channel. The Haruna logical gates (<code>logical_s</code>, <code>logical_hadamard</code>,
          <code>logical_cz</code>, <code>logical_t</code> and the Paulis) build gauge-field gates
          for a CSS code.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Verdicts</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>Projection</code>, the lattice of projections, and the Born read-out
          <code>born_projective_prob</code> that turns a measurement into a probability verdict.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>QPU seam</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>qpu_effect</code> lifts a device call into the causal monad, with
          <code>SimQpu</code> as the in-process stand-in. It needs the <code>qpu</code> feature.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section class="doc-sec" id="builds">
    <h2>Does it build without the standard library?</h2>
    <p>
      Yes, with a heap and without the QCL builder. CI builds the <code>no-std</code> feature alone
      for <code>thumbv7em-none-eabihf</code>. The QCL builder sits behind <code>qcm</code>, which
      implies <code>std</code>, so it stays on the host. No CI job builds the <code>qpu</code>
      feature for bare metal.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="table-stack">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Configuration</th><th scope="col">Supported</th><th scope="col">Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {configs.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Configuration"><b>{c.cfg}</b>{c.flags && <><br /><code>{c.flags}</code></>}</td>
              <td data-label="Supported">
                {c.ok ? <span class="status status-validated">yes</span> : <span class="status status-nongoal">no</span>}
              </td>
              <td data-label="Notes">{c.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Code code={nostd} lang="toml" themes={SHIKI} wrap />
    <p class="next-links">
      <a href={`${CRATE_URL}/README.md`}>The crate README ↗</a>
      <a href="/boundaries/">Boundaries →</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</PageShell>

<style>
  .table-scroll table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .table-scroll th, .table-scroll td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: var(--space-3); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-1); }
  .table-scroll thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: var(--t-eyebrow); letter-spacing: var(--ls-eyebrow);
    text-transform: uppercase; font-weight: 400; color: var(--fg-2); border-top: none;
  }
  .table-scroll td code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
</style>
